(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An 82-year-old on ciprofloxacin becomes confused and cannot sleep. What is the likely cause?","choices":["Serotonin syndrome","Anticholinergic delirium","Central nervous system toxicity","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dizziness, insomnia and somnolence, worse in the elderly. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 79"},{"q":"How are the neuromuscular blocking drugs described structurally?","choices":["Structural analogues of acetylcholine","As structural analogues of norepinephrine acting at adrenergic receptors","As enzymes that degrade acetylcholine at the endplate","As calcium channel blockers acting on the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both are used during surgery to produce complete muscle relaxation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A patient stops propranolol abruptly and develops arrhythmias. Why?","choices":["Accumulated metabolite","Withdrawal of the vehicle","Beta receptor up-regulation","Rebound alpha stimulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Never stop a beta blocker suddenly. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 117"},{"q":"Why are norepinephrine's actions diminished but not reversed by phenoxybenzamine?","choices":["Norepinephrine is not bound by alpha receptors","Norepinephrine is metabolised faster than epinephrine","Norepinephrine acts only on dopaminergic receptors","It lacks significant beta activity on vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so there is no vasodilating component left to unmask. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Aztreonam's spectrum resembles which class?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Macrolides","Glycopeptides","Nitroimidazoles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both cover Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterobacteriaceae, with no Gram-positive or anaerobic activity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What happens to benzoyl peroxide after it penetrates the stratum corneum?","choices":["It is converted to benzoic acid, which is active against Propionibacterium acnes","It is converted to salicylic acid, which is keratinolytic","It is converted to retinoic acid, which corrects keratinization","It remains unchanged in the skin and works purely by oxidising sebum within the follicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It crosses unchanged and is converted once through. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which receptor does dobutamine selectively stimulate?","choices":["Alpha-two","Beta-one","Beta-two","Alpha-one"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, a synthetic selective agonist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How does an alpha-two agonist such as clonidine lower blood pressure?","choices":["It reduces central sympathetic outflow","It blocks alpha-one receptors on vascular smooth muscle","It blocks beta-one receptors in the heart","It depletes vesicular norepinephrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so an agonist produces vasodilation by damping sympathetic drive. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What is the echinocandin mechanism of action?","choices":["Inhibits lanosterol demethylase, cutting ergosterol","Formation of membrane channels allowing potassium to leak from the cell","Disruption of the mitotic spindle, halting cell division","Inhibits 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin are the three agents, and the wall target is unique among the antifungals here. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"An exacerbation needs a medium to high potency steroid. What next?","choices":["Stop abruptly once the flare settles and use nothing further","Add an oral corticosteroid for the same duration","Use it for one to two weeks, then switch to a lower potency","Continue it indefinitely to prevent relapse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The step-down is the protocol rather than continuing at strength. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which drug is used for chronic management of myasthenia gravis?","choices":["Succinylcholine","Pyridostigmine","Edrophonium","Atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Edrophonium is for diagnosis, not maintenance. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient asks how long griseofulvin takes for a toenail infection. What do you say?","choices":["Up to 12 months","About 2 weeks","About 6 weeks","A single dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Scalp 1 month, fingernails 6 to 9, toenails up to 12. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 110"},{"q":"A patient on imiquimod has redness, swelling and erosions. What does this mean?","choices":["Irritation in nearly all; inflammation parallels efficacy","It indicates an allergic reaction and the drug should be stopped","It is unusual and suggests secondary bacterial infection","The dose is too low; increase the frequency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so the reaction is expected and is not by itself a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which is the major inhibitory transmitter in the brain?","choices":["Norepinephrine, which regulates normal brain processes","Gamma-aminobutyric acid","Glycine, the inhibitory transmitter of spinal cord neurons","Acetylcholine, which controls memory in brain neurons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while glycine is given as the inhibitory transmitter used by spinal cord neurons. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which herpesviruses are grouped as targets for antiviral therapy?","choices":["Varicella-zoster and hepatitis B","Herpes simplex, varicella-zoster and cytomegalovirus","Herpes simplex and influenza A only","Cytomegalovirus and human immunodeficiency virus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the first two share the acyclovir family, while cytomegalovirus needs ganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the topical azole antifungals?","choices":["They inhibit fungal cytochrome P450, preventing cell wall formation","They inhibit ergosterol production directly","They inhibit uptake of molecular precursors at the fungal cell wall","They bind fungal transfer RNA to stop protein synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the named agents are clotrimazole, ketoconazole, miconazole and sertaconazole. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient on intravenous acyclovir has a rising creatinine. What prevents this?","choices":["Good hydration","A loop diuretic","Alkalinising the urine","Slowing the infusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"It crystallizes in the renal tubule. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 123"},{"q":"How do albendazole and mebendazole work?","choices":["Block helminth microtubules and glucose uptake","Release acetylcholine and block cholinesterase","They inhibit ergosterol synthesis in the helminth membrane","They interact with parasite DNA to cause strand breakage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Note this sits outside the stated objectives, which name only antibacterials, antivirals and antifungals. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A patient using triple antibiotic ointment develops dermatitis around the wound. Which component?","choices":["The petrolatum base","Neomycin","Bacitracin","Polymyxin B"],"answer":1,"exp":"It frequently causes sensitization. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which antiviral does NOT act on viral nucleic acid?","choices":["Ganciclovir, which inhibits in a manner similar to acyclovir","Penciclovir, which has a mechanism similar to acyclovir","Oseltamivir, which inhibits neuraminidase to block viral release","Acyclovir, which is incorporated into viral DNA"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The guanine analogues all terminate the nucleic acid chain; oseltamivir works at a completely different stage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Why is acyclovir wrong for cytomegalovirus retinitis?","choices":["Acyclovir is intravenous only and unsafe in transplant","Acyclovir causes neutropenia in a third of patients","Acyclovir has no antiviral activity at all in immunocompromised patients","Acyclovir for simplex and zoster; ganciclovir for CMV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All are guanine analogues, but the indications are drawn along virus lines rather than by drug family. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which renal effect does acyclovir cause, and how is it prevented?","choices":["It causes interstitial nephritis requiring corticosteroids","Its intravenous vehicle accumulates in renal failure","It crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration and renal function must be maintained","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring hydration with normal saline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note how this differs from amphotericin B, which damages the tubule directly rather than crystallising in it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which fungal cell wall target has no human counterpart?","choices":["Ergosterol, which the polyenes bind","Lanosterol demethylase, which the azole agents inhibit","Microtubules, which griseofulvin disrupts","1,3-beta-D-glucan, whose synthase the echinocandins inhibit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mammalian cells have no cell wall at all, which makes this the cleanest selective target among the antifungals. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What happened to ephedra-containing herbal products?","choices":["They were banned in 2004","They were moved behind the pharmacy counter","They were reclassified as prescription only","They remain freely available"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What are griseofulvin's adverse effects?","choices":["Nephrotoxicity and electrolyte wasting","Visual disturbance in 30 percent with hepatic and renal effects","Headache, confusion, fatigue and blurred vision","CYP3A4 inhibition, raising substrate levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note it induces enzymes where the azoles inhibit them — opposite directions of interaction within the same broad category of drug. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which carbapenem lacks Pseudomonas coverage?","choices":["Doripenem","Ertapenem","Meropenem","Imipenem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The class covers Pseudomonas as a rule, and ertapenem is the stated exception. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient is starting a topical azole. How long is the course?","choices":["Treatment is generally prolonged, on the order of two to three weeks","A single application is usually curative","Treatment continues until the rash disappears, then stops immediately","Treatment must continue for at least six months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and stopping early is how a treated infection returns. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic is preferred for acne, and what is happening to the alternative?","choices":["Neomycin is preferred; polymyxin B is losing efficacy","Clindamycin preferred; erythromycin losing efficacy","Erythromycin is preferred; clindamycin is losing efficacy to resistance","Mupirocin preferred; bacitracin losing efficacy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and both are noted to lack systemic side effects. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient on an SSRI is started on linezolid and becomes agitated, febrile and tremulous. What is this?","choices":["Sepsis","Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Interacts with SSRIs, tyramine foods and pseudoephedrine. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 71"},{"q":"Why is pseudoephedrine kept behind the pharmacy counter?","choices":["It causes rebound congestion after three days","It requires blood pressure monitoring","It was banned by the Food and Drug Administration","It can be converted to methamphetamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is a regulatory rather than a pharmacological reason. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Once in viral DNA, how does acyclovir stop replication?","choices":["It inhibits DNA gyrase and prevents supercoiling","It is a guanine analogue lacking the sugar moiety, so the chain cannot elongate past it","It cross-links the two strands of viral DNA so that they can no longer separate","It causes helical structure loss and strand breakage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chain termination by a missing sugar, which is a structural rather than an enzymatic block. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"How should griseofulvin be taken to improve absorption?","choices":["With milk","With an antacid","With a high-fat meal","On an empty stomach"],"answer":2,"exp":"Absorption increases with fat. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is amphotericin B used against?","choices":["Helminths including hookworm and pinworm","Influenza A and B","The systemic mycoses; Aspergillus if invasive","Dermatophytes of skin, hair and nails only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Its breadth is why it remained the reference agent until voriconazole displaced it for aspergillosis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which gap in coverage does tolnaftate have?","choices":["It has no activity on the scalp","It has no Candida activity","It has no dermatophyte activity","It has no activity below the stratum corneum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and nystatin is the agent pairs with candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why do the noncatecholamines have longer half-lives?","choices":["They are not substrates for the inactivating enzymes","They are protein bound and released slowly from plasma","They are stored in vesicles and released over hours","They are excreted unchanged by the kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and their greater lipid solubility also increases central penetration. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How does pyrantel pamoate kill its target parasites?","choices":["It blocks neuraminidase and prevents the parasite from leaving host cells","Releases acetylcholine and blocks cholinesterase","It inhibits formation of helminth microtubules and blocks glucose uptake","Inhibits squalene epoxidase, depleting ergosterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is used for pinworm and hookworm. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What are posaconazole's adverse effects?","choices":["QT prolongation, fever, diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and thrombocytopenia","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients","Headache in about 15 percent of patients, with mental confusion and blurred vision","Bone marrow suppression and hepatotoxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The QT effect places it alongside the macrolides and fluoroquinolones as a drug to watch in combination. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What are the three general types of signalling between cells?","choices":["Neurotransmitters, receptors and second messengers","Afferent, efferent and enteric signalling","Hormones, local mediators and neurotransmitters","Hormones, enzymes and ion channels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the neurotransmitters of interest here are acetylcholine, norepinephrine and epinephrine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why is succinylcholine suited to rapid sequence intubation?","choices":["Its long duration, which covers the whole procedure","Its lack of any cardiovascular effect","Its reversibility with neostigmine","Its rapid onset and short duration of action"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with the short duration owing to rapid breakdown by plasma enzyme. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What distinguishes scopolamine from atropine?","choices":["It has no central effects and acts purely peripherally","It stimulates muscarinic receptors rather than blocking them","It is a selective bladder agent","Greater central action, preventing motion sickness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with sedation at lower doses and excitement at higher ones. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which organism defeats the penicillinase-resistant penicillins, and what replaces them?","choices":["Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus, for which linezolid is the treatment of choice","Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase producing organisms, for which carbapenems are preferred","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, for which cefepime is preferred","MRSA, for which vancomycin is the treatment of choice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The class was built for Staphylococcus aureus and methicillin resistance is precisely what defeats it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which amphotericin B formulation is the conventional one?","choices":["Amphotericin B lipid complex, marketed as Abelcet","Liposomal amphotericin B, marketed as AmBisome","Amphotericin B colloidal suspension, marketed as Amphotec","Amphotericin B deoxycholate, marketed as Fungizone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The three lipid alternatives are the lipid complex, the colloidal suspension and the liposomal form. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which hypertensive crisis is phentolamine used for?","choices":["Crisis in thyroid storm","Abrupt clonidine withdrawal","Crisis in pre-eclampsia","Crisis from cocaine intoxication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the rebound the clonidine slide warns about. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which muscarinic receptor subtypes are functionally characterised?","choices":["M1 and M2 only","M2 and M4 only, the cardiac subtypes","M1, M2 and M3 only, of five subclasses","All five subclasses, M1 through M5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. M4 and M5 are listed as neuronal without further characterisation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the allylamine mechanism of action?","choices":["Inhibition of glucan synthesis in the cell wall","Inhibition of squalene epoxidase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","Inhibition of lanosterol demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","Binding ergosterol already present and forming membrane channels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same end point as the azoles, reached at an earlier step of the pathway. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"What is cefazolin used for?","choices":["Meningitis and neonatal sepsis","Complicated intra-abdominal and anaerobic infection","Surgical prophylaxis, MSSA and urinary tract infection","Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin covers a similar niche orally, aimed at cellulitis and urinary tract infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which agents treat Alzheimer disease, and why?","choices":["Atropine, scopolamine and glycopyrrolate, because they reduce secretions","Pralidoxime and dantrolene, because they restore enzyme function","Donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine","Physostigmine, neostigmine and pyridostigmine, because they cross into the brain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and their common adverse effect is gastrointestinal distress. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What warning applies to polymyxin B?","choices":["Avoid in pregnancy; absorption through inflamed skin","Avoid it in children under eight because it stains teeth","Avoid it with benzoyl peroxide because it is inactivated","Not on open wounds in high doses; neuro and nephrotoxicity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loss of the barrier is what turns a topical agent into a systemic exposure. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How does atropine's cardiovascular effect vary with dose?","choices":["Tachycardia at lower doses and bradycardia at higher doses","Bradycardia at every dose","No cardiac effect, since its action is confined to secretions","Bradycardia at lower doses and tachycardia at higher doses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is given during a code to treat bradycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How do levofloxacin and moxifloxacin differ in coverage?","choices":["Levofloxacin covers atypicals; moxifloxacin does not","Moxifloxacin requires renal adjustment; levofloxacin does not","Levofloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; moxifloxacin does not","Moxifloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; levofloxacin does not"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both improve on ciprofloxacin's Gram-positive activity and both cover community-acquired pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which beta-lactamase inhibitor is paired with piperacillin?","choices":["Sulbactam, marketed as Unasyn","Clavulanate, marketed as Augmentin","Avibactam, marketed as Avycaz","Tazobactam, marketed as Zosyn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sulbactam pairs with ampicillin as Unasyn, and clavulanate with amoxicillin as Augmentin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Linezolid is drug of choice for what, and what does it miss?","choices":["Anaerobic intra-abdominal infection; it does not cover aerobes","Atypical pneumonia; it does not cover Staphylococcus","Hospital and community MRSA, with no Gram-negative cover","Pseudomonal and resistant Gram-negative infection only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It also covers multidrug-resistant pneumococcus and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How does vancomycin block cell wall synthesis, unlike a penicillin?","choices":["It inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks transpeptidation","It disrupts the outer membrane by displacing magnesium and calcium ions","Binds the D-alanine residues to block cross-linking","Covalently inactivates the penicillin-binding protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin mimics the substrate and disables the enzyme; vancomycin covers the substrate itself, which is why resistance is harder to develop. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which systemic effects do topical corticosteroids cause?","choices":["Hepatotoxicity, nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","Raised serum lipids and brittle nails","Photosensitivity, sunburn, desquamation and stinging","Adrenal suppression, hyperglycaemia, glaucoma, cataracts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That a topical agent can do all of this is the point of the potency and duration warnings. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which muscles does a nondepolarizing blocker paralyse first?","choices":["The small muscles of the face and eye","The diaphragm and intercostal muscles","The muscles of the trunk and neck","All skeletal muscle simultaneously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the diaphragm goes last, with recovery in reverse order. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A child needs a topical steroid for facial eczema. Which potency?","choices":["Any potency is fine","Low potency","Superpotent","High potency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Low potency for face, intertriginous areas and infants. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 37"},{"q":"Within what window must oseltamivir be started?","choices":["At any point during the illness, with equal benefit","As soon as possible, within 48 hours of symptom onset","Within 7 days of symptom onset","Within the first 24 hours, as with acyclovir in chickenpox"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because it blocks release rather than killing virus already present, late treatment has little to act on. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"What are dopamine's adverse effects?","choices":["Tremor, restlessness and anxiety","Sedation and dry mouth","Bronchoconstriction","Nausea, hypertension and arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What is the risk of stopping clonidine abruptly?","choices":["Rebound bronchospasm","Adrenal suppression","Rebound nasal congestion","Rebound hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is tapered rather than stopped. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What is clindamycin drug of choice for?","choices":["Toxin-mediated disease, skin, bone, penicillin allergy","Pseudomonal pneumonia and febrile neutropenia","Urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis","Meningitis, cerebral abscess and neurosurgical prophylaxis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The toxin-mediated indication follows from inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis and therefore toxin production. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"}]};
